cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  ctsegdose-core: anatomy-weighted CTDIvol from routine CT metadata, using
  deep-learning organ segmentation
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it, and cite ctdose-core for the dose
  engine it builds on.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Yamamoto
    given-names: Shuji
    email: yamamoto@lisit.jp
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9211-1071'
    affiliation: Institute of One, LISIT Co., Ltd., Tokyo 150-0044, Japan
repository-code: https://github.com/Institute-of-One/ctsegdose-core
url: https://github.com/Institute-of-One/ctsegdose-core
abstract: >-
  A Python package that computes an anatomy-weighted CTDIvol index from routine
  CT DICOM metadata and automated organ segmentation: the whole-scan CTDIvol
  scaled by a dimensionless, organ-specific weight formed from the recorded
  per-slice tube current over that organ's segmented longitudinal extent. Organ
  volume and attenuation-derived estimated organ mass are reported alongside.
  The quantity is not new -- it is the organ-specific weighted CTDIvol of
  Khatonabadi et al. (2013) and Tian et al. (2015); what this software provides
  is an open, end-to-end implementation that runs from archived DICOM alone,
  with contours obtained at inference. It is not an estimate of absorbed organ
  dose: the index describes longitudinal tube-current modulation only, and no
  organ-dose coefficient table is redistributed. No imaging is redistributed;
  the public series analysed are identified by Series Instance UID, collection
  and licence so they can be re-fetched.
keywords:
  - computed tomography
  - CTDIvol
  - tube current modulation
  - organ segmentation
  - TotalSegmentator
  - image-based dosimetry indices
  - DICOM
  - reproducibility
license: MIT
version: 0.1.2
# The *concept* DOI: it resolves to the latest version, which is what a citation of the
# project should point at. A result should cite the version DOI of the release it was
# produced with, so the paper names the exact snapshot it was run on.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21817234
